# Communications.  Nothing here can dial anything -- there is no modem and
# no peer -- so what is photographed is each program's own face: the prompt,
# the settings, the log it keeps.
shot    kermit
cap     C-Kermit 5A at its own prompt, showing the file-transfer settings it
cap     would use.  There is no line to dial here; this is the program
cap     itself, not a transfer.
run     /dd/CMDS/REBUILT/kermit
wait    6
send    show protocol\r
wait    5
send    exit\r
wait    3

shot    aterm
cap     ATerm 2.6, the terminal emulator, reading its configuration from
cap     SYS/ATERM.
run     /dd/CMDS/aterm
wait    6

shot    uuname
cap     uuname lists the UUCP sites this system could reach, out of the
cap     configuration in SYS.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/uuname
wait    4
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/uulog
wait    4

shot    mailx
cap     mailx opening with no mail waiting -- which is the honest state of
cap     a mail reader on a disk with no network.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/mailx
wait    5
send    q\r
wait    3

shot    readnews
cap     readnews, the news reader, with no spool to read.
run     /dd/CMDS/UUCP/readnews
wait    5
send    q\r
wait    3

shot    setterm
cap     setterm and cls, the two smallest things here that know what a
cap     terminal is.
run     /dd/CMDS/setterm
wait    4
